# CLIP and LLaVA — Vision-Language Lab

## Overview

This project explores vision-language models through image classification,
image-text similarity, semantic image retrieval, visual question answering,
and hallucination analysis.

## Repository Contents

- Jupyter Notebook: completed experiments, code, outputs, answers, and remarks.
- images/: images used in the experiments.
- requirements.txt: Python packages used for the project.

## Experiments

1. CLIP model loading and image preprocessing
2. Image-text similarity
3. Zero-shot image classification
4. Classification accuracy evaluation
5. Prompt engineering
6. Top-5 text-to-image retrieval
7. LLaVA visual question answering
8. Human evaluation of VQA answers
9. Visual hallucination analysis
10. CLIP/LLaVA comparison
11. Mini-project: image-based question-answering assistant

## Reported Results

- CLIP classification: 10 correct predictions out of 10 images (100%).
- LLaVA VQA: 20 correct answers out of 25 questions (80%).
- A hallucination example involving an absent bicycle is included.

These results apply only to the small test sets used in this lab.

## How to Run

1. Open the notebook in Google Colab.
2. Enable a GPU runtime.
3. Run the installation cells provided in the notebook.
4. Upload the images and check the image paths.
5. Run the experiment cells in order.

The original experiments used the directory /bin/images.
Adapt the paths or recreate this directory before running the notebook.

## Limitations

Generated answers may contain incorrect or invented details.
Human verification is required.
